228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 480 0 R
/E (The paper builds a machine learning model that predicts which pull requests \(PRs\) are most likely to fail. Using this prediction, it reorders the merge queue, letting the safer PRs run first and delaying risky ones.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 481 0 R
/E (To do this, it uses data like code changes, number of commits, reviews, and past CI test results. The model \(XGBoost\) is trained on past PR data and tested to see how well it predicts failures.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 482 0 R
/E (Then, the researchers simulate real merge scenarios and compare their method with normal FIFO ordering and simple rules. Their approach merges more PRs successfully and reduces pipeline delays)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
